        "original_id": "4cab773e-0c76-54d6-b4c0-7a8e31b51aa0",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Francis Kinloch Huger, Gilbert du Motier Marquis de Lafayette, George Washington, Alexander Hamilton, Thomas Jefferson\n- Places: South Carolina, United States\n- Roles: physician, artillery officer, legislator, aristocrat, military officer\n- Events: American Revolutionary War, French Revolution, July Revolution, November 1794 rescue attempt\n- Products: La Fayette (film)\n\nFacts:\n- Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette, was born on 6 September 1757 and died on 20 May 1834.\n- Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette, was a French aristocrat and military officer who fought in the American Revolutionary War.\n- Francis Kinloch Huger was born on September 17, 1773 and died on February 14, 1855.\n- Francis Kinloch Huger was a trained physician and artillery officer and was a member of the Huger family of South Carolina.\n- In November 1794, Francis Kinloch Huger led a failed attempt to rescue Lafayette from captivity during the wars surrounding the French Revolution.\n- Lafayette was a close friend of George Washington, Alexander Hamilton, and Thomas Jefferson.\n- Lafayette was a key figure in the French Revolution of 1789 and the July Revolution of 1830.\n- Francis Kinloch Huger served in the South Carolina House of Representatives and the South Carolina Senate.\n- In the United States, Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette, is often known simply as Lafayette.\n- The film La Fayette depicts the life of Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette, especially his role in the American War of Independence.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "ef9fced0-453c-5291-b184-de0c1a021822",
        "text": "Page: Francis Kinloch Huger. Sentence: Colonel Dr. Francis Kinloch Huger (September 17, 1773 – February 14, 1855), a trained physician and artillery officer, was a scion of the Huger family of South Carolina.\nPage: Francis Kinloch Huger. Sentence: A member of the South Carolina House of Representatives and South Carolina Senate, he is best known for his leadership of a failed November 1794 attempt to rescue Lafayette from captivity during the wars surrounding the French Revolution.\nPage: Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette. Sentence: Marie-Joseph Paul Yves Roch Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette (] ; 6 September 1757 – 20 May 1834), in the United States often known simply as Lafayette, was a French aristocrat and military officer who fought in the American Revolutionary War.\nPage: Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette. Sentence: A close friend of George Washington, Alexander Hamilton, and Thomas Jefferson, Lafayette was a key figure in the French Revolution of 1789 and the July Revolution of 1830.\nPage: La Fayette (film). Sentence: The film depicts the life of Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette, in particular his role in the American War of Independence.",
